<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Diego</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a>–<a href="/years/1548">1548</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Juan Diego Cuauhtlatoatzin, Saint Juan Diego</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Diego was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> convert to whom, according to Catholic tradition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a> appeared on the hill of Tepeyac near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in December 1531. The image said to have been imprinted on his cloak became the focus of the Guadalupe devotion. He was canonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> in 2002 as the first indigenous saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-diego.jpg" alt="Juan Diego" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Cabrera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MIguel_Cabrera_-_Fiel_retrato_do_vener%C3%A1vel_Juan_Diego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to major sources Juan Diego was born in 1474 in Cuauhtitlan, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>, and lived there or in Tolpetlac at the time of the apparitions. He was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peasant, neither destitute nor rich or influential. He and his wife, María Lucía, were among the first to be baptised after the main group of twelve Franciscan missionaries arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1524. After the apparitions he was allowed to live beside the hermitage at the foot of Tepeyac and devoted the rest of his life to serving the Virgin at the shrine. His death is given as 1548.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The tradition, as told in the Nican mopohua, holds that at dawn on Wednesday 9 December 1531 the Virgin Mary appeared to him at Tepeyac and told him to ask Bishop Juan de Zumárraga to build a chapel in her honour. The bishop asked him to return later. Meeting her again, Juan Diego suggested she choose someone of greater standing, but she insisted on him. On 10 December the bishop asked for a sign.</p><p>When Juan Diego's uncle Juan Bernardino fell sick, he tried to avoid the hill, but the Virgin met him and asked, &quot;Am I not here, I who am your mother?&quot; She told him to gather flowers blooming out of season on the rocks. He carried them in his mantle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and when he opened it the image of the Virgin was found imprinted on it. Juan Bernardino was found recovered. On 26 December 1531 the image was taken in procession to a small chapel at Tepeyac.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Nican mopohua was first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> in 1649, after Miguel Sánchez's Spanish account of 1648, and Antonio Valeriano is traditionally considered its author. Historians dispute Juan Diego's historicity, chiefly because Zumárraga and the earliest ecclesiastical historians are silent about him, while others reply that silence does not prove he did not exist. Clear evidence of a Marian cult at Tepeyac appears by 1556.</p><p>The Basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Our Lady of Guadalupe</a> at the foot of the hill houses the cloak traditionally said to be his. He was beatified on 6 May 1990. The miracle accepted for canonisation was the recovery of Juan José Barragán Silva, who fell from a balcony in Querétaro that month. John Paul II canonised him in 2002, the first indigenous American saint.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Diego” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church was the central institution of cultural life, and the devotion to the Virgin of Guadalupe, said to have appeared to <b>Juan Diego</b> in 1531, became a shared focus of identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2019 the tenor <b>Juan Diego</b> Flórez sang it at La Scala in Milan, accompanying himself on the guitar, in a performance watched 17 million times on YouTube.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cucurrucucu-paloma">Cucurrucucú paloma</a></li><li><blockquote>According to tradition, the Virgin Mary appeared to the Indigenous convert <b>Juan Diego</b> on the hill of Tepeyac near Mexico City.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cuautitlán, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Tepeyac Hill, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.67,-99.17&amp;year=1522" aria-label="Cuautitlán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1435 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Diego"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.67,-99.17&amp;year=1522">What was here in 1522? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
